README.md
     3→# Swarm (experimental, educational)
     5→> [!IMPORTANT]
     6→> Swarm is now replaced by the [OpenAI Agents SDK](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python), which is a production-ready evolution of Swarm. The Agents SDK features key improvements and will be actively maintained by the OpenAI team.
     7→>
     8→> We recommend migrating to the Agents SDK for all production use cases.
     9→
    10→## Install
    12→Requires Python 3.10+
    13→
    14→```shell
    15→pip install git+ssh://git@github.com/openai/swarm.git
    16→```
    17→
    18→or
    19→
    20→```shell
    21→pip install git+https://github.com/openai/swarm.git
    22→```
    23→
    24→## Usage
    26→```python
    27→from swarm import Swarm, Agent
    28→
    29→client = Swarm()
    30→
    31→def transfer_to_agent_b():
    32→    return agent_b
    33→
    34→
    35→agent_a = Agent(
    36→    name="Agent A",
    37→    instructions="You are a helpful agent.",
    38→    functions=[transfer_to_agent_b],
    39→)
    40→
    41→agent_b = Agent(
    42→    name="Agent B",
    43→    instructions="Only speak in Haikus.",
    44→)
    45→
    46→response = client.run(
    47→    agent=agent_a,
    48→    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "I want to talk to agent B."}],
    49→)
    50→
    51→print(response.messages[-1]["content"])
    52→```
    53→
    54→```
    55→Hope glimmers brightly,
    56→New paths converge gracefully,
    57→What can I assist?
    58→```
    59→
    60→## Table of Contents
    72→# Overview
    74→Swarm focuses on making agent **coordination** and **execution** lightweight, highly controllable, and easily testable.
    75→
    76→It accomplishes this through two primitive abstractions: `Agent`s and **handoffs**. An `Agent` encompasses `instructions` and `tools`, and can at any point choose to hand off a conversation to another `Agent`.
    77→
    78→These primitives are powerful enough to express rich dynamics between tools and networks of agents, allowing you to build scalable, real-world solutions while avoiding a steep learning curve.
    79→
    80→> [!NOTE]
    81→> Swarm Agents are not related to Assistants in the Assistants API. They are named similarly for convenience, but are otherwise completely unrelated. Swarm is entirely powered by the Chat Completions API and is hence stateless between calls.
    82→
    83→## Why Swarm
    85→Swarm explores patterns that are lightweight, scalable, and highly customizable by design. Approaches similar to Swarm are best suited for situations dealing with a large number of independent capabilities and instructions that are difficult to encode into a single prompt.
    86→
    87→The Assistants API is a great option for developers looking for fully-hosted threads and built in memory management and retrieval. However, Swarm is an educational resource for developers curious to learn about multi-agent orchestration. Swarm runs (almost) entirely on the client and, much like the Chat Completions API, does not store state between calls.
    88→
    89→# Examples
    91→Check out `/examples` for inspiration! Learn more about each one in its README.
    92→
    93→- [`basic`](examples/basic): Simple examples of fundamentals like setup, function calling, handoffs, and context variables
    94→- [`triage_agent`](examples/triage_agent): Simple example of setting up a basic triage step to hand off to the right agent
    95→- [`weather_agent`](examples/weather_agent): Simple example of function calling
    96→- [`airline`](examples/airline): A multi-agent setup for handling different customer service requests in an airline context.
    97→- [`support_bot`](examples/support_bot): A customer service bot which includes a user interface agent and a help center agent with several tools
    98→- [`personal_shopper`](examples/personal_shopper): A personal shopping agent that can help with making sales and refunding orders
    99→
   100→# Documentation
   104→## Running Swarm
   106→Start by instantiating a Swarm client (which internally just instantiates an `OpenAI` client).
   107→
   108→```python
   109→from swarm import Swarm
   110→
   111→client = Swarm()
   112→```
   113→
   114→### `client.run()`
   126→#### Arguments
   141→#### `Response` Fields
   149→## Agents
   151→An `Agent` simply encapsulates a set of `instructions` with a set of `functions` (plus some additional settings below), and has the capability to hand off execution to another `Agent`.
   152→
   153→While it's tempting to personify an `Agent` as "someone who does X", it can also be used to represent a very specific workflow or step defined by a set of `instructions` and `functions` (e.g. a set of steps, a complex retrieval, single step of data transformation, etc). This allows `Agent`s to be composed into a network of "agents", "workflows", and "tasks", all represented by the same primitive.
   154→
   155→## `Agent` Fields
   157→| Field            | Type                     | Description                                                                   | Default                      |
   158→| ---------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
   159→| **name**         | `str`                    | The name of the agent.                                                        | `"Agent"`                    |
   160→| **model**        | `str`                    | The model to be used by the agent.                                            | `"gpt-4o"`                   |
   161→| **instructions** | `str` or `func() -> str` | Instructions for the agent, can be a string or a callable returning a string. | `"You are a helpful agent."` |
   162→| **functions**    | `List`                   | A list of functions that the agent can call.                                  | `[]`                         |
   163→| **tool_choice**  | `str`                    | The tool choice for the agent, if any.                                        | `None`                       |
   164→
   165→### Instructions
   197→## Functions
   199→- Swarm `Agent`s can call python functions directly.
   200→- Function should usually return a `str` (values will be attempted to be cast as a `str`).
   201→- If a function returns an `Agent`, execution will be transferred to that `Agent`.
   202→- If a function defines a `context_variables` parameter, it will be populated by the `context_variables` passed into `client.run()`.
   203→
   204→```python
   205→def greet(context_variables, language):
   206→   user_name = context_variables["user_name"]
   207→   greeting = "Hola" if language.lower() == "spanish" else "Hello"
   208→   print(f"{greeting}, {user_name}!")
   209→   return "Done"
   210→
   211→agent = Agent(
   212→   functions=[greet]
   213→)
   214→
   215→client.run(
   216→   agent=agent,
   217→   messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Usa greet() por favor."}],
   218→   context_variables={"user_name": "John"}
   219→)
   220→```
   221→
   222→```
   223→Hola, John!
   224→```
   225→
   226→- If an `Agent` function call has an error (missing function, wrong argument, error) an error response will be appended to the chat so the `Agent` can recover gracefully.
   227→- If multiple functions are called by the `Agent`, they will be executed in that order.
   228→
   229→### Handoffs and Updating Context Variables
   281→### Function Schemas
   321→## Streaming
   323→```python
   324→stream = client.run(agent, messages, stream=True)
   325→for chunk in stream:
   326→   print(chunk)
   327→```
   328→
   329→Uses the same events as [Chat Completions API streaming](https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/streaming). See `process_and_print_streaming_response` in `/swarm/repl/repl.py` as an example.
   330→
   331→Two new event types have been added:
   332→
   333→- `{"delim":"start"}` and `{"delim":"end"}`, to signal each time an `Agent` handles a single message (response or function call). This helps identify switches between `Agent`s.
   334→- `{"response": Response}` will return a `Response` object at the end of a stream with the aggregated (complete) response, for convenience.
   335→
   336→# Evaluations
   338→Evaluations are crucial to any project, and we encourage developers to bring their own eval suites to test the performance of their swarms. For reference, we have some examples for how to eval swarm in the `airline`, `weather_agent` and `triage_agent` quickstart examples. See the READMEs for more details.
   339→
   340→# Utils
   342→Use the `run_demo_loop` to test out your swarm! This will run a REPL on your command line. Supports streaming.
   343→
   344→```python
   345→from swarm.repl import run_demo_loop
   346→...
   347→run_demo_loop(agent, stream=True)
   348→```
   349→
